<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pope Zachary</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/752">752</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a> · Also known as Pope Zacharias</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pope Zachary was a Greek-speaking pope from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calabria">Calabria</a> who held office from 741 to 752. He negotiated with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombard</a> kings to protect <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and supported the church reforms of Boniface in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francia">Francia</a>. When Pippin the Short asked whether the man with real power should be king, Zachary approved, and in 751 Pippin deposed the last Merovingian, Childeric III, beginning the Carolingian alliance with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">papacy</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pope-zachary.jpg" alt="Pope Zachary" width="320" height="524" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sailko · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Et%C3%A0_di_papa_Zaccaria,_cappella_del_primicerius_Teodoro,_Madonna_in_trono_con_Theodoto_e_la_moglie,_741-752_(09).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Zachary was born in 679 into a family of Greek origin in the Calabrian town of Santa Severina. He was most probably a deacon of the Roman Church and as such signed the decrees of the Roman council of 732. He was chosen to succeed Gregory III on 3 or 5 December 741, and he was the last pope of the Byzantine Papacy.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>Gregory III's alliance with the Lombard Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spoleto">Spoleto</a> had put papal cities at risk when the dukes of Spoleto and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benevento">Benevento</a> rebelled. Zachary turned to King Liutprand the Lombard directly, and out of respect for him the king restored the territory the Lombards had seized and sent back captives without ransom. At the request of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/exarchate-of-ravenna">Exarchate of Ravenna</a> he persuaded Liutprand to abandon a planned attack on the city. The Liber pontificalis stresses his personal influence with Liutprand and with his successor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ratchis">Ratchis</a>.</p><p>He corresponded with Archbishop <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-boniface">Boniface of Mainz</a>, confirmed the new bishoprics of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wurzburg">Würzburg</a>, Büraburg and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erfurt">Erfurt</a>, and in 742 made Boniface papal legate to the Concilium Germanicum. In 745 he convened a synod in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> to discourage the worship of angels. Asked by the Frankish Mayor of the Palace Pepin the Short whether it was better that the man with the royal power or the man without it should be king, he answered that it was better for the one who held the power, and soon afterwards the Frankish nobles abandoned the last Merovingian king, Childeric III. He also protested to the Byzantine emperor Constantine V about his iconoclastic policy.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Zachary built the original church of Santa Maria sopra <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minerva">Minerva</a> near the Pantheon, over an ancient temple of Minerva, and restored the Lateran Palace. After Venetian merchants bought many slaves in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> to sell to the Muslims of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, he forbade the traffic and paid the merchants their price, so that the slaves were freed.</p><p>He died on 15 March 752 and was buried in St Peter's Basilica. His elected successor, Stephen, died within days, so that Stephen II followed. The church historian Johann Peter Kirsch called him an excellent, capable, vigorous and charitable successor of Peter, and Peter Partner called him a skilled diplomat in a dark corridor of history.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pope Zachary” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Santa Severina, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=39.15,16.92&amp;year=742" aria-label="Santa Severina on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4969 760 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Pope Zachary"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5419 1030h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5419 1030h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=39.15,16.92&amp;year=742">What was here in 742 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
